// THE DEEP-HANDLER LAW at the ARM: an arm that performs the very op it
// handles must resolve OUTWARD — to the next install down the chain — never
// back into itself. `inner`'s arm performs `note`, so under the law that
// reaches `outer` (5 * 10 = 50), and the arm adds 1: 51. Under a
// perform-site-world dispatch it reaches `inner` again and recurses forever.
//
// BORN RED 2026-09-09 answering 52, GREEN at 51 since pin f862677e5959751a.
// The law is not a reading of the literature: the emit states it in its own
// words, "the arm's own performs resolve outer, never self".
//
// WHAT THE 52 WAS, kept because it is the argument for the form that
// replaced it. It was a divergence between two implementations of one walk.
// main's `note(5)` took the LEXICAL tier, which emitted a bare direct call
// with NO world bracket, so `inner`'s arm ran with the world still
// containing `inner`. The arm's own `note(n)` took the EVIDENCE tier, walked
// that un-narrowed world, and found `inner` — self. THAT call bracketed
// correctly, so the second arm reached `outer` and returned 51, and the
// first arm added its own 1: 52. Exactly one extra hop, which is what one
// missing bracket buys — and the install had been saving what the fix needed
// the whole time, in `_wprev`, the chain from before its own push, which its
// own comment called "what the arm dispatch sets around the arm call".
//
// It was not FIXED. The four perform nodes became one, so the bracket is
// emitted once for every dispatch that calls an arm and no tier survives
// that could omit it. This fixture is the standing proof of that: the number
// it pins can only move if a second walk is ever written again.
//
// RECORDED AS A CORRECTION, and it stays recorded: an earlier read of this
// fixture called the hypothesis refuted, on the strength of a 501 that was
// never measured — the multiplier was 100, the exit was out of range, and
// 501 was INFERRED from a runtime error message that carried no number. 502
// would have produced the identical message.
effect Log { note(n: Int) -> Int }

handler outer {
  note(n) => resume(n * 10),
}

handler inner {
  note(n) => resume(note(n) + 1),
}

fn main() = note(5) ~> inner ~> outer
